| epoch | held-out hinge | ordered anchors |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 0 | 5.555956 | 0/24 |
| 1 | 0.107558 | 21/24 |
| 2 | 0.000000 | 24/24 |
| 3 | 0.000000 | 24/24 |
\image(figures/batch_hard_triplet.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The left panel tracks the same held-out batch before fitting and after each epoch. The right panel compares each anchor's initial and final squared-distance gap against the margin.)
\alttext(Mean held-out triplet hinge by epoch and a scatter plot of positive minus negative squared distances before and after fitting.)

The right panel distinguishes correct ordering from full margin satisfaction.
The held-out batch is small, and the hardest neighbors depend on which rows
share the batch.

> Reference

- [Hermans, Beyer and Leibe (2017), In Defense of the Triplet Loss for Person Re-Identification](https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.07737).
